{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqatnwimnk0","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/zonoid","title":"Defensibility = learned conditional inject policy, no fixed prior; neural gate is the product","summary":"Strategy synthesis (2026-06-12, user-stated), supersedes the abstain-by-default framing. The empirical record proves the inject policy must be CONDITIONAL: v2-v7 (self-solvable tasks by construction) showed inject HURTS (over-deliberation tax) — fixed winner was never-inject; the current held-out suite (KB-dependent tasks by construction, the v4 pivot) shows abstain HURTS (0% locale-sum) — fixed winner is always-inject. Each era crowned the fixed policy matching its task distribution; real traffic is a mixture varying by repo and KB maturity, so NO fixed prior (neither abstain-by-default nor always-inject) is the product. The product is the learned conditional boundary: p(inject helps | task, note, KB state) trained on shadow-journal rows joined with outcomes ([[note-mqathh2z9c4]], [[note-mqasdxx0tnt]]). The original claim 'retrieve only when it helps' stands — the abstain-by-default PRIOR is what failed. Feature consequence: the 5 affinity features are insufficient — the boundary needs TASK-SIDE features (self-solvability; the model-routing hook's per-prompt complexity score is free and already computed; task length, spec presence) and KB-STATE features (note density near query, KB size/maturity, empirical fraction — a 0.55 cosine means different things in a 12-note vs 300-note KB, the unmodeled topical-cluster effect). Extend the journal schema with these BEFORE volume accumulates — retroactively impossible, same logic as the embed version stamp.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-12T11:07:16.702Z","vec":[-0.08388503640890121,-0.09904950112104416,0.010152878239750862,0.012166219763457775,0.03560013324022293,0.07563085108995438,0.06669694185256958,0.02689998783171177,-0.02692791447043419,0.051995836198329926,0.03648054972290993,0.03144795075058937,-0.02085411